                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "41017096-9ca6-43e3-97a9-fcf0ee95f986",
                        "source_entry_id": "2930bce7-c12a-37f2-a0df-4fd141e586c4",
                        "source_sense_id": "1a725a63-d1bd-3b8a-b944-f1bcd29dd7d9",
                        "source_sense_description": "Mathematik: nullteilerfreier kommutativer Ring mit vom Nullelement verschiedenem Einselement",
                        "target_lang_word": "integral domain",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "integral domain",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "cdddd84d-2ea6-3369-8cce-9c06cc145fb7",
                                        "entry_id": "8da5168f-dd47-3334-af55-538dacaa96ef",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any nonzero commutative ring in which the product of nonzero elements is nonzero."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "07d11275-49e7-44bc-b138-a0bb34669181",
                                                "sense_id": "cdddd84d-2ea6-3369-8cce-9c06cc145fb7",
                                                "text": "A ring R is an integral domain if and only if the polynomial ring R#91;x#93; is an integral domain."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6c01b7ee-7c49-41e8-b586-4023dd6394e4",
                                                "sense_id": "cdddd84d-2ea6-3369-8cce-9c06cc145fb7",
                                                "text": "For any integral domain there can be derived an associated field of fractions."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "00d14e66-1746-4541-817c-32c01bcc957f",
                                                "sense_id": "cdddd84d-2ea6-3369-8cce-9c06cc145fb7",
                                                "text": "For integral domains, we will use a⁻¹ to designate the multiplicative inverse of a (if it has one; since not all elements need have inverses, this notation can be used only where it can be shown that an inverse exists)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bdad69bb-b9f4-43a3-8de9-60bfff5600b0",
                                                "sense_id": "cdddd84d-2ea6-3369-8cce-9c06cc145fb7",
                                                "text": "An integral domain is said to have strong pseudo-Dedekind factorization if each proper ideal can be factored as the product of an invertible ideal (possibly equal to the ring) and a finite product of pairwise comaximal prime ideals with at least one prime in the product."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1dc3603b-793e-46f5-bfe4-c03f37433a27",
                                                "sense_id": "cdddd84d-2ea6-3369-8cce-9c06cc145fb7",
                                                "text": "#91;#92;mathbb#123;Z#125;#59;#43;,#92;cdot#93;, #91;#92;mathbb#123;Z#125;#95;p,#43;#95;p,#92;times#95;p#93; with p a prime, #91;#92;mathbb#123;Q#125;#59;#43;,#92;cdot#93;, #91;#92;mathbb#123;R#125;#59;#43;,#92;cdot#93;, and #91;#92;mathbb#123;C#125;#59;#43;,#92;cdot#93; are all integral domains. The key example of an infinite integral domain is #91;#92;mathbb#123;Z#125;#59;#43;,#92;cdot#93;. In fact, it is from #92;mathbb#123;Z#125; that the term integral domain is derived. Our main example of a finite integral domain is #91;#92;mathbb#123;Z#125;#95;p,#43;#95;p,#92;times#95;p#93;, when p is prime."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "fddbff5d-a653-4f79-8690-13ee66bca839",
                        "source_entry_id": "2930bce7-c12a-37f2-a0df-4fd141e586c4",
                        "source_sense_id": "1a725a63-d1bd-3b8a-b944-f1bcd29dd7d9",
                        "source_sense_description": "Mathematik: nullteilerfreier kommutativer Ring mit vom Nullelement verschiedenem Einselement",
                        "target_lang_word": "anneau intègre",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "anneau intègre",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "882b7516-3d25-3b60-8f0a-67e5f4166e07",
                                        "entry_id": "8c2b0407-4292-3c07-b565-4b3de2f8c5e8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Anneau commutatif unitaire non vide et sans diviseur de zéro."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5bc8e77b-7341-4d1b-a130-9d3d13e5c92c",
                                                "sense_id": "882b7516-3d25-3b60-8f0a-67e5f4166e07",
                                                "text": "L'ensemble des nombres entiers, muni de l'addition et la multiplication, forme un anneau intègre."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
